 "notes": "Blind pass, no key used. Images: P1370706 = open bifolium (left page all cipher letter text L1-L22; right page clear closing, date 'A Paris le 23 Aoust 1651', signature Louis de Bourbon, address-like note 'M. du Chambon' at foot). P1370710 = single page photographed rotated 90 deg (read after rotating 90 deg clockwise); dated top-left '6 Aoust 1651'; archive stamp at lower right partly covers the last cipher group of L25. P1370711 = open bifolium: left page = continuation of a letter (no salutation at top) with cipher, closing, signature, then a 2-line postscript with cipher (L19-L20); right page has 4 clear lines ending 'Le 8 Aoust 1651'.\nConventions/decisions: (1) SYM:sigma used for the round 'G/6-like' glyph whose top stroke runs out horizontally to the right (e.g. 706 L2 '57 [sigma] 33', 711 L11); it is clearly distinct from the digit 6 of this hand (which has a rising ascender, as in 26, 16, 86). Where I first read it as a '6' with a bar I marked SYM:sigma?. Small closed loops with a short top tick (710 L9, L12, L19, L21, L22, 711 L12) also given as SYM:sigma?. (2) SYM:mars = loop with a stroke rising to the upper right ending in an arrowhead (710 L7, L11 marked mars?; 711 L8 clear). (3) SYM:pi = low wide bar on short legs; SYM:Pi = taller 'TT' form; the two often appear side by side (706 L2, 710 L18). (4) SYM:autre-II-barre = 'II' between a top and a bottom bar (Roman-numeral-II-like), seen at 706 L1, 710 L21, 711 L19. (5) SYM:livre used for the t/d-like glyph with double crossbar (706 L1 certain; 710 L12, L19 marked livre?). (6) SYM:autre-alpha (710 L22, twice: line-initial 'alpha' could also be a clear French 'a'; second one forms a ligature-like group 'alpha+psi' - split as two uncertain tokens). (7) '8?' at 710 L25 and 711 L12 is an open S-shaped glyph read as 8 (could be a symbol). (8) Bars over 82 and 83 at 706 L7 are crisp straight unhooked bars -> macron?. Bars over 81 (706 L5) and 46 (711 L11) are straight but not clearly higher -> tilde(?).\nInsertions/corrections: 710 L25: a group after 73 is struck out and blotted; the interlinear '71.29.38' written above it is given as its own cipher run in that position (71 has two heavy dots above). 711 L13: a small '20' with tilde written above the end of 'Maintenant' is given as its own run (interlinear; could alternatively be an abbreviation mark). 710 L7 and L11: one short word struck out at line start (not transcribed). 710 L10: a struck word before 'la Chamesouvrier'. 711 L5: a struck-out group at line start (looks like 4 digits with an acute), not tokenised. 711 R4: 'faire' struck with a correction written above.\nOther doubts: 706 L2 first token '11' with a small hook above; second token's first digit is overwritten/blotted (42 vs 32). 706 L6 '21?' (second digit could be 2). 706 L17 '37?' (could be 34). 706 L22 single '1' with dot(s) above. 710 L10 '94?' at end of clear text, 9 seems overwritten. 710 L16 '99' carries a short vertical stroke flanked by two dots above (marked other?). 711 L6 first '96' has a heavy oblique bar above (other?). 711 L8: a tall vertical stroke between 66 and 48, entered as SYM:autre-trait-vertical? (may be a separator/insertion mark, not a token). 711 L19 'SYM:tilde-seul' = free-standing tilde after a q-barré. Single dots over 1/7/9 entered as diaeresis? per instructions. Faint diaeresis on many tokens judged at ~3-4x zoom; some may be missed or be specks. Clear text is a rough anchor reading only."
